> It's absurd because language doesn't actually work that
> way.  Well, computer languages do, but we're talking about
> human languages here.  People who already know how to say
> what they want to say don't consult dictionaries to find
> out if they're saying it in the right way.  People consult
> dictionaries to find what they *don't* already know, and
> they want to know what the author of whatever they are
> reading meant by what they wrote.  Hence, dictionaries list
> the various ways in which words are used (and good
> dictionaries include examples), so that the reader can
> figure out which meaning the author was using and what thus
> know what he meant.  If the dictionary does not include the
> meaning that the author intended (e.g., because of a
> misguided attempt to keep the language "pure" by not
> including some meanings), then the reader won't be able to
> figure out the sentence.  If this happens often, the
> reader's going to give up on that dictionary and look for
> a better one.

I give you an example, a quite common type, if you want to
express things appropriately. In German we have two words,
'offensichtlich' and 'offenbar'; if I look into dictionaries
I get (as the prominent choice) 'obviously' in _both_ cases.
Transcribing texts that way will lose expressiveness and
will not explain the issue accurately. My plea is to try to
keep language meaning at the high level, thus being able to
differentiate issues. Another type of example is 'anarchy';
while from the original meaning it would mean 'absence of
governmental authority' it evolved (based on *political*
reasons) to the (primarily, but not only, popular) meaning
of 'chaos'.

